Use `$md` to turn a template body into HTML. A blank line separates a
heading from the paragraph below it. Two stars on each side make text bold.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    summary = [$md:
        # Build report

        All tests **passed**.
    ]
]

The result contains a heading and a paragraph. For inline code, use one
backtick on each side of the text. For a code listing, use a nested
`$code` template. Moth Markdown supports lists and links. Use a template
when you need a table.

### Link to a section @#section-ids

Add a section marker to give a heading a link target. The marker itself stays
out of the rendered text.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    guide = [$md:
        ## Install @#install

        Jump back to @#install (Install).
    ]
]

The heading displays `Install`. The link targets it by the explicit ID
`install`. An unmarked heading has no ID.
